course: world
lesson: 172
title: Loot boxes, streaks and the sunk cost
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In his game, Ollie opens loot boxes (paid surprise boxes with a random prize) for a legendary skin. The chance is 1 in 100 per box. He opens 100 boxes and gets nothing. "That's impossible! 100 boxes should give me one!" But 1 in 100 is a chance, not a promise.
explain: With a 1 in 100 chance per box, after 100 boxes about two players in three have at least one legendary, and about one in three still have none. Ollie is unlucky, not cheated, unless the game promised a guarantee.
explain: Sam has spent twenty pounds of birthday money and got nothing good. "I can't stop now, I've already spent twenty quid." That is the sunk cost trap: the money is gone whatever he does next. The only real question is whether the next box is worth it.
explain: Other traps hide in boxes too: a "lucky third pack", a "hot streak", "better drops at midnight", a streamer who shows only his best openings, and calling paid chance a "surprise". Many experts say loot boxes work like gambling.
explain: Siblings take the mickey out of the maths, not the gamer: "It's a 1 percent chance, not a 1 percent promise."
fact: A 1 in 100 chance per box means each box has a small chance; it does not promise one prize in every 100 boxes.
fact: With a 1 in 100 chance, after 100 boxes about two players in three have at least one prize, and about one in three still have none.
fact: The sunk cost fallacy is spending more because you have already spent; money spent is gone whatever you do next.
fact: Random draws often come in clumps by chance; two lucky boxes in a row don't change the next box's chance, and thinking they do is the hot-hand fallacy.
fact: Each pack or box has the same chance; there is no "lucky third pack".
fact: Videos show the exciting moments; seeing only the winners and not the losers is called survivorship bias.
fact: Games use random number generators (a part of the program that picks by chance), and there is no evidence that the time of day changes the chances; testing it would need hundreds of openings.
fact: Many experts say loot boxes work like gambling, because you pay money for a random chance at a prize, and some countries have rules about them.
fact: Our brains remember disappointments more than good luck; keeping a simple tally shows the real numbers.
fact: In many games, items cannot be sold for real money, so they are worth nothing outside the game.
fact: Some games publish their drop chances, so you can look them up instead of guessing.
fact: Words people use: "drops" are the items a game gives you, "a skin" is a new look for a character, "quid" means pounds (UK money), "galaxy brain" is sarcastic for a silly idea, "a vibe" is a feeling, and "bestie" means best friend.
exercise: The chance of a legendary skin is 1 in 100 per box. What does that mean? | Each box has a small chance; nothing is promised | Box number 100 always has the skin | One box in every 100 is sure to have it | The chance rises a little bit with every box you open, until you get it
exercise: Out of every three players who each open 100 such boxes, about how many still get nothing? | About one | None of them | All three | About two
exercise: Ruby got the rare card in her third pack and says the third pack is always lucky. What is true? | Each pack has the same chance | The third pack is always the lucky one | The first pack is the luckiest | Packs get luckier the more you buy
exercise: Noah watched a streamer open 50 packs with loads of rare cards. What is he missing? | The videos show the best moments, not every pack | The packs really are better for streamers | He should buy exactly 50 packs | Rare cards are only ever given out to famous people with lots of fans
exercise: Sam has spent twenty pounds and says he can't stop now. What is this trap? | The sunk cost fallacy | The hot-hand fallacy | A fair investment | Survivorship bias
exercise: What is the only real question for Sam before the next box? | Is the next box worth it on its own? | How much money has he spent so far on boxes? | Will it win back all of his twenty pounds? | Are his friends all still buying boxes too?
exercise: Ellie tried opening boxes at midnight twice and got one good item. What is wrong with her proof? | Two tries is far too few to show anything | Midnight is simply the luckiest time of day | She should try once at noon too, and then stop | One good item already proves that it works
exercise: Tom gets two rare items in a row and keeps opening. What happens? | He gets nothing for the next 40 | He keeps getting rare items | He gets a rare item every second box | The game gives him a bonus streak
exercise: What do grown-ups call believing luck runs in streaks? | The hot-hand fallacy | The sunk cost fallacy | A fair test | Confirmation bias
exercise: Maya says loot boxes are just surprises, not gambling. What do many experts say? | They work like gambling: pay money, random prize | They are exactly like birthday presents | They are always free surprises | They are a kind of savings account, so the money is still yours later
exercise: Leo says he never gets good drops. What does his friend show? | A screenshot of Leo's three good drops last week | That the game really hates Leo | That Leo never plays the game | That nobody in the whole world ever gets any good drops at all
exercise: Why does Leo remember only the bad drops? | Brains remember disappointments more than good luck | The good drops were not real | The game deletes good memories | Good drops only ever happen at night, when Leo is already asleep
exercise: Arjun hoped to sell his rare skin for money. What did he find out? | The game doesn't allow it to be sold | It was worth a fortune | Rare skins sell for more than games | He could swap it for a new console
exercise: Priya's friend can "feel" that paying players get better drops. What should she do instead? | Look up the game's published drop chances | Trust the feeling, because feelings like that are usually right | Pay more to test the feeling | Ask the friend to feel harder
exercise: Where do the "drops" in a game come from? | A random number generator in the game | The exact time of day that you open them | How badly the player really wants them | How many of your friends are online then
test: His sister: "It's a 1 percent chance, not a 1 percent promise. Bro bought 100 tickets to disappointment." Who is she teasing? | Ollie's belief that chance is a promise, not Ollie himself | The game company for being honest | Ollie for liking skins | Her own luck with boxes
test: Her brother: "So I should just buy the third pack first? Galaxy brain." Why is that funny? | The "third" pack is just whichever pack you open third | Third packs are really cheaper | He really thinks it would work | Galaxy packs are rarer
test: Noah's mate: "The guy opened 1000 packs and posted the best 50. You saw the highlights, not the whole film." What trap is this? | Only seeing the winners | Moving the goalposts | Spending more because you spent | A trick question
test: His older sister: "Mate, that's how you lose £40 instead of £20." What would happen if Sam stopped now? | He would lose no more than the £20 already gone | He would lose the £20 he already spent all over again | The game would notice and refund all of his money | He would get a free legendary skin for being loyal
test: Ellie's brother: "You tried it twice. That's not science, that's a vibe." What would a real test need? | Hundreds of openings at different times, counted | Two more tries at midnight | Asking friends what they think | One try at midnight, then one try at noon, and compare the two
test: His cousin: "Hot streak? You've been cold for 40 boxes, bro. Freezer streak." What is true? | Two lucky boxes didn't change the next box's chance | Hot streaks always last ten boxes | Cold streaks mean a win is due very soon, so he should keep going | The game turned off his luck
test: Mum: "Paying money and hoping for a prize? Call it a surprise all you like, it's still a gamble, sweetheart." What is she saying? | Changing the name doesn't change what it is | Surprises are always bad | Loot boxes are free | Gambling is only for adults with bank cards, so this can't be gambling
test: Friend: "The game hates you? It gave you a golden dragon on Tuesday, you ungrateful goblin." Is "goblin" a real insult here? | No, it's friendly teasing about forgetting the good drops | Yes, it's meant to really hurt | Yes, goblins are bad players | No, because Leo really is a goblin character in the game he plays
test: His dad: "So you bought a really rare thing you can't sell. Congratulations on your very expensive picture." What should Arjun have done first? | Checked the game's rules before spending | Bought even more boxes to get a better one | Asked a famous streamer to buy it from him | Sold it to a friend for cash straight away
test: Priya: "You can feel it? Bestie, that's not data, that's a mood." What does "bestie" mean? | Best friend | Best player | The best drop | A boss in the game
test: In a cartoon a robot says, "After 99 misses, box 100 MUST be a winner!" Put it right. | Box 100 has the same small chance as every other box | Box 100 is always a winner | Box 100 is always empty | After 99 misses the game must refund you
exam: Identify the principal misconception in the expectation that one hundred boxes, each offering a one-in-one-hundred chance, must yield one prize. | A probability is mistaken for a guarantee | The number of boxes was insufficiently large | The prize was incorrectly described | The boxes were opened at the wrong hour
exam: Determine the term properly applied to continued expenditure motivated by the sum already expended. | The sunk cost fallacy | The hot-hand fallacy | Survivorship bias | Appeal to authority
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning items obtained in many games. | They frequently cannot be sold for real money | They are invariably exchangeable for currency | Their value is fixed by law | They increase in value with rarity in all cases
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the inference drawn from a streamer's video displaying numerous rare cards? | It rests upon a selection of successes and omits the failures | It is a representative sample of all openings | It is a controlled experiment | It establishes the published drop chances
